<p>President Trump said Tuesday that the United States would pause “Project Freedom,” its effort to guide stranded commercial vessels through the Strait of Hormuz, one day after the mission began, while negotiations with Iran continued. He said Pakistan and other countries had requested the pause and cited “great progress” toward an agreement with Tehran, but said the U.S. blockade of Iranian ports would remain in force. The announcement followed public statements earlier Tuesday by Secretary of State Marco Rubio, Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth and U.S. Central Command, who described the escort operation as active or newly underway. Rubio said the U.S. military campaign called “Operation Epic Fury” had ended and that the United States had moved to “Project Freedom,” while Hegseth said U.S. efforts to free trapped vessels would continue. The Strait of Hormuz, a major route for energy shipments, has been largely blocked during the U.S.-Iran conflict, with some reports saying the closure affected about 20% of world oil supplies. Tehran had no immediate public reaction after Trump’s post, and the White House did not immediately provide details on the reported progress or say how long the pause would last.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/S3CShhacL5GKxQ9J7NsSQIdb1FpLCu0HLK0rW7YPkcFk8gujZ0bYo5J6I-ujJ_8F965Zfhu3HScfDLARzLtgLb6xf1Fu_cc6Okv6SvVAO-8wzzOaEgPtlXZ1hQBJpF2p5R_74jrSAaSRGfNn4pUMVocpKEwHFrQsSsmRmDhltTMyq-Y70Q' alt='An Iran-flagged container vessel anchored in the Strait of Hormuz off Bandar Abbas in southern Iran on May 2, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>United Nations: Rubio urged China and Russia not to veto a U.S.-backed Security Council text drafted with Bahrain, Saudi Arabia, the UAE, Kuwait and Qatar on Iranian attacks and mines in the strait.</li></ul><blockquote>Project Freedom (The Movement of Ships through the Strait of Hormuz) will be paused for a short period of time to see whether or not the Agreement can be finalized and signed - President Donald Trump</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Negotiating context: Politico reported that Iran had demanded the U.S. blockade be lifted before it would negotiate an end to its nuclear program; Trump said the blockade would remain in force. (<a href='https://www.politico.com/news/2026/05/05/trump-announces-a-pause-on-us-escorts-of-ships-in-strait-of-hormuz-00907622'>Politico</a>)</li><li>U.S. military posture: DW reported that, after the pause, the U.S. said it would operate defensively to enforce the naval blockade rather than continue the ship-movement operation. (<a href='https://www.dw.com/en/iran-war-trump-says-project-freedom-paused/live-77059422'>DW</a>)</li><li>Iranian state media: Iranian state media portrayed the U.S. pause as a diplomatic victory and said Trump had retreated after failing to fully reopen the strait. (<a href='https://minutemirror.com.pk/trump-pauses-hormuz-escort-mission-amid-iran-talks-551096/'>Minute Mirror</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://en.mercopress.com/2026/05/06/just-one-day-after-its-launch-trump-halts-hormuz-reopening-operation-and-turns-to-negotiations-with-iran'>Just one day after its launch, Trump halts Hormuz reopening operation and turns to negotiations with Iran</a> - mercopress.com</li><li><a href='https://www.nytimes.com/live/2026/05/05/world/iran-war-trump-hormuz'>Trump Again Shifts U.S. Focus on Strait, Pausing Day-Old Escort Mission</a> - nytimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.ft.com/content/5f7ba94e-8a42-4f36-8c4c-19be932c8f66'>Trump pauses US plan to guide ships through Strait of Hormuz</a> - ft.com</li><li><a href='https://www.cbsnews.com/news/trump-pauses-u-s-mission-to-guide-ships-through-strait-of-hormuz-project-freedom/'>Trump pauses Project Freedom in Strait of Hormuz to see if Iran deal can be struck</a> - cbsnews.com</li><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/middle-east/trump-says-pausing-hormuz-operation-in-push-for-iran-deal'>US pauses Hormuz escorts in bid f